# ==== T-FMT-C · statement-position match — suite-layout form preservation
#
# AXIS (7 cells): case inline-call · case inline-`throw` · case inline-`return`
#   · case next-line x2 · else next-line x2.
#
# ⚠ There is NO `else:`-inline cell: `match x:` / `else: print(2)` is a PARSE
#   ERROR (measured 2026-08-11 — `expected NEWLINE, found identifier`), so the
#   statement-match `else` clause has exactly one legal form. That is why the
#   `Stmt::Match` else site carries NO layout read: an inline branch there
#   could never be reached, and a cell for it could never go RED.
#
# RED pre-fix (recorded 2026-08-11):
#   * case inline-`throw`  — EXPLODED to a next-line body. The parser wraps a
#                            `throw`/`return` expression-prefix in a SYNTHETIC
#                            `Expr::Block`, and `format_match_arm`'s
#                            `if let Expr::Block(..)` shape test could not tell
#                            it from an author-written indented suite.
#   * case inline-`return` — same, same mechanism.
# GREEN pre-fix / BREAK-AND-WATCH: `case 1: g(1)` (a bare call is NOT wrapped,
#   so it never matched the if-let) and the four next-line cells. Flip
#   `format_match_arm` to ignore `Block.layout` and the next-line cells collapse.
#
# ⚠ TODO.md:1584 claimed "statement-position match: unaffected". That claim was
#   FALSE at HEAD — two of its seven cells were live. Disproof recorded here so
#   the premise is not re-filed from the old entry.

void g(int y):
    print(y)

int f(int x):
    match x:
        case 1: g(1)
        case 2: return 5
        case 3: throw "bad"
        case 4:
            g(4)
        case 5:
            int t = x + 1
            g(t)
        else:
            g(9)
    return 0

int h(int x):
    match x:
        case 1:
            g(1)
        else:
            int u = x + 2
            g(u)
    return 1

void main():
    print(f(1))
    print(h(1))
